      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "why": "unrolled_k is the same shared-datapath area class as iterative but issues k micro-rotations/cycle, cutting result latency to ceil(N/k)+3 cycles. This is the key lever for the system constraint: a 1 MHz tick dumps 32 requests at once and all 32 must return within 0.5 us, so the per-result latency and the burst drain rate dominate. Sweeping k=2..8 maps the latency-vs-area trade-off (higher k = more parallel shift/add logic = more area, but faster burst drain). Same accuracy brackets as iterative so the two fronts are directly comparable on the accuracy objective."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Only iterative and unrolled_k are available to me, and both are shared-datapath (smallest area class), so both are the right place to find the min-area design. The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) depends only on data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding, so I bracket those tightly: data_width 14-18 and n_iter 12-16 are the region where CORDIC error crosses 2^-12, with guards 0-2 and both rounding modes to let Optuna find the cheapest accurate point. The real tension in this spec is the system constraint: a 1 MHz tick issues 32 requests simultaneously and all 32 must be back within 0.5 us (p99 over ticks), plus a 32 MS/s throughput floor. iterative (1 result per N+3 cycles) is the area floor but its burst-drain latency is the risk; unrolled_k trades area for k-fold faster drain. I weight unrolled_k slightly heavier (0.6) because the p99-batch bound is the binding system constraint and k is the only knob that attacks it, while keeping 0.4 on iterative to anchor the area floor and confirm whether it can pass at all. This split maps the area-vs-burst-latency front across both families within the 100-eval budget."
}

